Abstract

Designed as an entire full stack messaging solution in real-time, this project utilizes Spring Boot as a Java-based backend, React as the front-end framework, and PostgreSQL as the database solution. The project addresses three major issues that remain in Popular Messaging Systems: loss of messages (this being silent) while the user is offline, lack of delivery transparency to the sender, and the lack of an ability for Users and Organizations to host their own instance of the system and be in control of all their own data.

Platforms like WhatsApp, Slack and Telegram uses a centralised closed system which although works well for general users, they lack visibility into the ways their offline messages are managed and don't provide any structured mechanism for the delivery of messages or access to run their service through their own infrastructure. These users may think their messages did not reach their intended recipient because of no notification from the recipient or response from the other end. This app will address the issues mentioned above, so it would be the answer to all.

The foundation of the system is based on the ability to deliver messages using a store and forward type of architecture. Messaging is written to the database before attempting delivery to ensure that messaging is not lost due to network failures from either side of the network. Real-time communication between clients occurs through authenticated session-based WebSockets which permits the immediate sharing of messages when both clients are connected. If a client is not connected to the system when another member sends a message to that client, the system will hold the message until such time that the client reconnects to the system. Once the client reconnects, the client will receive all waiting messages in the order in which they were sent (chronological order).

To ensure that senders are aware of the state of messages that they have sent, the system features a three-state message delivery system: once a server has received a message it is considered to be in a pending state, after a client (the recipient) has received the message it is considered to be in a delivered state, and after the recipient opens the message it becomes readable or "read". In addition, our system enables all three of these states to be displayed to the sender in real time to eliminate the ambiguity of message delivery that exists on other platforms.

User authentication is managed in a secure fashion, by using passwords that are hashed and salted, and also using sessions that use tokens which do not last very long. Users will be authenticated between browser sessions and will not have to log in every time. Web socket connections will have to be validated with credentials before they can create sessions.

This project is built using a carefully chosen set of modern tools. The frontend is developed in React.js, providing a fast and responsive user interface. The backend runs on Spring Boot, handling all business logic and API management. PostgreSQL is used as the relational database to store users, messages, and conversation data. WebSocket enables real-time two-way communication between client and server. REST APIs handle standard operations like login, registration, and message history retrieval. Hibernate serves as the ORM layer, mapping Java objects directly to database tables, making data management cleaner and more efficient throughout the application.

This first version is one-on-one texting only, but registered users can send each other texts. It has all its own infrastructure, so it fits the needs of people and businesses who want full control of their data and privacy. It is also built out to have the ability to expand later to go horizontally beyond just the one server, which is the starting point.

In conclusion, this chat application brings together a reliable and modern full stack architecture to solve real problems that existing platforms overlook. The expected output is a fully functional, self-hostable messaging platform where no message is ever lost, delivery status is always transparent, and users or organizations retain complete control over their own data. This project serves as a strong demonstration of full stack development skills using industry-standard tools and is designed with future scalability in mind.
